WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:03.403
(instrumental)

00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:20.229
- [Narrator] Mankind and the elements.

00:00:20.229 --> 00:00:23.130
For some, it's an uncomfortable bond.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.709
- In bad conditions,
it can be a death zone.

00:00:26.709 --> 00:00:28.133
- [Narrator] For others,

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.831
when weather strikes, inspiration begins.

00:00:30.831 --> 00:00:32.533
- [Casey] When you build
an instrument out of it,

00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:34.293
you're kind of bringing
that tree back to life

00:00:34.293 --> 00:00:35.850
because it's creating music.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.947
- [Narrator] These are the
people who challenge nature,

00:00:37.947 --> 00:00:41.989
seek out its limits, reveal its secrets,

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:44.489
and embrace its awesome power.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.650
In this episode, we'll meet a team of

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.169
imaginative Foley artists
creating sounds of

00:00:51.169 --> 00:00:52.950
weather for motion pictures.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.011
- [Chris] Your ear hears snow,

00:00:55.011 --> 00:00:57.572
but in actuality, it's sand.

00:00:57.572 --> 00:00:59.951
- [Narrator] A Hawaiian family who's made

00:00:59.951 --> 00:01:02.590
world class ukuleles from
rare Koa trees for decades.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.490
- Not only a beautiful wood,

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:07.289
but kind of symbolizes the family.

00:01:07.289 --> 00:01:09.311
- [Narrator] And two
brothers who bike in some of

00:01:09.311 --> 00:01:11.631
the most extreme
environments known to man.

00:01:11.631 --> 00:01:13.647
- [Joe] It's steep and it's gnarly,

00:01:13.647 --> 00:01:15.407
and that's just an extra bit of danger,

00:01:15.407 --> 00:01:17.450
which is always a plus.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.402
- [Narrator] These pioneers
of the great outdoors

00:01:19.402 --> 00:01:21.402
ahead on That's Amazing.

00:01:33.185 --> 00:01:35.905
In Hollywood, everything
is magic and make believe.

00:01:35.905 --> 00:01:38.347
- [Alyson] Gloves with
paperclips are dog paws.

00:01:38.347 --> 00:01:40.220
- [Narrator] Even what you hear.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.043
- [Alyson] We are storytellers with sound.

00:01:42.043 --> 00:01:46.390
Over here we have feather
duster we use for bird wings.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:47.786
We bring sound into the story

00:01:47.786 --> 00:01:51.990
and we can create any
mood with that sound.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.787
- [Narrator] When you watch a film,

00:01:53.787 --> 00:01:55.787
much of what you're hearing was created,

00:01:55.787 --> 00:01:58.370
from footsteps on a snowy path,

00:01:59.887 --> 00:02:02.230
to a vicious thunderstorm.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:06.069
- [Alyson] Weather is a
really interesting thing

00:02:06.069 --> 00:02:07.409
to recreate.

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:08.827
- Make it exciting.

00:02:08.827 --> 00:02:11.008
- [Alyson] It depends on the
elements that we're using

00:02:11.008 --> 00:02:13.427
and how it's filmed and
what they wanna hear.

00:02:13.427 --> 00:02:15.270
- [Narrator] Here at
the Warner Brothers lot,

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.089
Foley artists practice their craft,

00:02:17.089 --> 00:02:19.009
and if they do their jobs right,

00:02:19.009 --> 00:02:21.110
you won't even notice.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.629
- [Alyson] People take sound for granted,

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:25.409
but you would miss it if it wasn't there.

00:02:25.409 --> 00:02:28.992
(sand crunching underfoot)

00:02:33.723 --> 00:02:36.443
- [Alyson] Foley is the art of sound.

00:02:36.443 --> 00:02:41.179
It's creating sounds in sync
with what's happening onscreen.

00:02:41.179 --> 00:02:44.581
The art of Foley goes
back to the old radio days

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:47.737
where you would see the
sound guys with their,

00:02:47.737 --> 00:02:49.942
clomping with their coconuts.

00:02:49.942 --> 00:02:51.419
This is what we use for horse hooves.

00:02:51.419 --> 00:02:54.203
They're plungers, and we stuff 'em with

00:02:54.203 --> 00:02:57.997
cloth and a little tape, and here we go.

00:02:57.997 --> 00:03:00.923
(galloping)

00:03:00.923 --> 00:03:02.390
Jack Foley came in and he thought,

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.455
"If I do those sounds in
sync with the picture,"

00:03:06.455 --> 00:03:08.841
"I've just taken a whole step out."

00:03:08.841 --> 00:03:12.283
- [Narrator] Jack Foley's legacy
in sound began in the 1920s

00:03:12.283 --> 00:03:14.681
as talkies swept Hollywood.

00:03:14.681 --> 00:03:17.176
Ever since, the reproduction
of sound effects

00:03:17.176 --> 00:03:22.139
added to film in post-production
is called the art of Foley.

00:03:22.139 --> 00:03:25.222
- The Foley stage consists
of different surfaces.

00:03:25.222 --> 00:03:28.502
- This is walking on
leaves or in a forest.

00:03:28.502 --> 00:03:32.002
(rain falling, footsteps)

00:03:34.139 --> 00:03:37.414
For snow, what we normally
use is regular play sand,

00:03:37.414 --> 00:03:39.403
and then to add to that,

00:03:39.403 --> 00:03:41.799
sometimes you hear that familiar crunch,

00:03:41.799 --> 00:03:44.523
which we use cornstarch.

00:03:44.523 --> 00:03:46.923
(cornstarch crunching underfoot)

00:03:46.923 --> 00:03:51.006
Your ear hears snow, but
in actuality, it's sand.

00:03:59.227 --> 00:04:01.126
- [Alyson] It's almost like a prop house.

00:04:01.126 --> 00:04:03.686
We have collected different things,

00:04:03.686 --> 00:04:05.787
because everything here
should make a sound.

00:04:05.787 --> 00:04:08.370
Here's a classic squeaky hinge.

00:04:09.329 --> 00:04:13.102
Pine cones can be used for cracking ice.

00:04:13.102 --> 00:04:16.142
- You have the three people
that are on the stage,

00:04:16.142 --> 00:04:18.204
the mixer and the two Foley artists,

00:04:18.204 --> 00:04:22.343
which it's important that you
have a team that's cohesive.

00:04:22.343 --> 00:04:24.583
- You know, something's cracking, popping.

00:04:24.583 --> 00:04:25.809
I don't know what it is.

00:04:25.809 --> 00:04:27.182
- Is it this?

00:04:27.182 --> 00:04:28.865
(leaves rustling)

00:04:28.865 --> 00:04:30.849
- [Chris] Sometimes it takes
a little trial and error

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:32.827
to see what works best.

00:04:32.827 --> 00:04:36.186
- [Alyson] We have a streamer
that goes across the screen

00:04:36.186 --> 00:04:38.326
and when the streamer gets to the end,

00:04:38.326 --> 00:04:42.246
that tells us when that
sound should start.

00:04:42.246 --> 00:04:45.166
It's an interesting dance.

00:04:45.166 --> 00:04:49.126
There's really nothing
that we can't do here

00:04:49.126 --> 00:04:50.543
imagination-wise.

00:04:52.928 --> 00:04:54.584
- [Mary Jo] Right now
we're in the control room,

00:04:54.584 --> 00:04:55.966
and it's where I mix,

00:04:55.966 --> 00:05:00.145
and I listen to them
performing to the picture.

00:05:00.145 --> 00:05:01.885
I try not to look at them,

00:05:01.885 --> 00:05:04.107
because I really don't wanna
see what they're using.

00:05:04.107 --> 00:05:06.440
I want to see how it sounds.

00:05:09.243 --> 00:05:12.160
(thunder crashing)

00:05:13.286 --> 00:05:17.025
- All sound is two elements
hitting one another.

00:05:17.025 --> 00:05:18.886
- [Alyson] With rain,
if it's hitting a puddle

00:05:18.886 --> 00:05:21.339
or if it's hitting someone's face,

00:05:21.339 --> 00:05:22.941
that's all gonna sound different.

00:05:22.941 --> 00:05:26.705
So those are very specific little things

00:05:26.705 --> 00:05:29.185
that we have to watch and look at.

00:05:29.185 --> 00:05:31.352
Okay, Mary Jo, here we go.

00:05:32.842 --> 00:05:35.925
(water hitting boot)

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:54.262
Wind is interesting because it's usually

00:05:54.262 --> 00:05:57.122
what the wind is blowing through.

00:05:57.122 --> 00:06:00.604
- Alyson's gonna use the
Batman cape to create the sails

00:06:00.604 --> 00:06:02.681
and I'm going to create the boat

00:06:02.681 --> 00:06:04.886
actually going through the water.

00:06:04.886 --> 00:06:06.136
Okay, let's go.

00:06:08.005 --> 00:06:12.140
(water splashing, fabric rustling)

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:14.182
- [Alyson] If we're doing
a boat going through waves,

00:06:14.182 --> 00:06:17.206
we would use the big tub that we have

00:06:17.206 --> 00:06:19.243
to get that slapping sound,

00:06:19.243 --> 00:06:23.410
or churning up the water a
lot in the tub with our hands.

00:06:24.726 --> 00:06:27.243
- [Mary Jo] A lot of sound,

00:06:27.243 --> 00:06:30.422
like a lot of things
artistic, it’s very subjective.

00:06:30.422 --> 00:06:32.966
So, I have to make the judgment call.

00:06:32.966 --> 00:06:36.683
There's a great moment when
you get the perfect sound,

00:06:36.683 --> 00:06:39.446
and they don't always know it, but I do.

00:06:39.446 --> 00:06:40.740
How was that, Mary Jo?

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:41.963
- [Mary Jo] Sounded great.

00:06:41.963 --> 00:06:44.200
- When there are multiple
weather elements involved,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.763
we might do all the specific things first

00:06:46.763 --> 00:06:50.596
and then go back and do
maybe a general sound.

00:06:51.821 --> 00:06:55.862
We have a shot here of a hiker
coming down the waterfall,

00:06:55.862 --> 00:06:57.643
so I'm doing the sound of the carabiner

00:06:57.643 --> 00:07:01.025
and his rope and his equipment.

00:07:01.025 --> 00:07:03.775
(metal clanking)

00:07:07.665 --> 00:07:08.683
How was that?

00:07:08.683 --> 00:07:09.686
- [Mary Jo] That was cool.

00:07:09.686 --> 00:07:10.519
- Nice.

00:07:15.694 --> 00:07:18.313
(water hitting concrete)

00:07:18.313 --> 00:07:19.956
- So, I'm gonna play it back all together

00:07:19.956 --> 00:07:21.433
and see what we've got.

00:07:21.433 --> 00:07:24.433
(waterfall rushing)

00:07:26.574 --> 00:07:28.036
- [Alyson] We really layer everything so

00:07:28.036 --> 00:07:31.732
the mixers can mix how they wanna hear it,

00:07:31.732 --> 00:07:34.494
and we do things also that
are a little over the top

00:07:34.494 --> 00:07:38.661
because we have to fight
with music, sometimes dialog,

00:07:41.172 --> 00:07:44.047
but hopefully when you hear it at home,

00:07:44.047 --> 00:07:47.556
it'll sound just like
it should be natural.

00:07:47.556 --> 00:07:50.556
(waterfall rushing)

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:00.953
- [Narrator] On Eil Malk Island in Palau,

00:08:00.953 --> 00:08:03.453
there is a lake like no other.

00:08:04.607 --> 00:08:07.940
(soft electronic music)

00:08:09.491 --> 00:08:11.694
It's called Jellyfish Lake.

00:08:11.694 --> 00:08:15.092
This stunning saltwater lake
is roughly 12,000 years old

00:08:15.092 --> 00:08:18.092
and was once connected to the ocean.

00:08:19.133 --> 00:08:22.873
But as the island changed,
it was closed off.

00:08:22.873 --> 00:08:24.373
Now, in isolation,

00:08:25.934 --> 00:08:29.156
it is filled with a jellyfish
species found nowhere else,

00:08:29.156 --> 00:08:30.906
the golden jellyfish.

00:08:33.556 --> 00:08:35.054
These cryptic creatures spend their days

00:08:35.054 --> 00:08:38.852
migrating across the lake,
following the path of the sun,

00:08:38.852 --> 00:08:42.754
and with no predators
to keep them in check,

00:08:42.754 --> 00:08:46.921
the jellyfish swarm and fill
the lake like a cloudy dream.

00:08:50.494 --> 00:08:51.854
It's safe for humans to swim here

00:08:51.854 --> 00:08:54.436
because though the
jellyfish do have stingers,

00:08:54.436 --> 00:08:58.313
they are too small to be felt by humans.

00:08:58.313 --> 00:09:02.612
Gliding through this
place feels otherworldly.

00:09:02.612 --> 00:09:05.195
(instrumental)

00:09:12.174 --> 00:09:14.174
Grass is something we take for granted.

00:09:14.174 --> 00:09:15.673
We walk all over it, lay on it,

00:09:15.673 --> 00:09:17.840
and sometimes mow it down.

00:09:19.572 --> 00:09:21.432
Most of us don't give it much thought.

00:09:21.432 --> 00:09:24.612
Few of us appreciate it the way

00:09:24.612 --> 00:09:28.212
that Dan Harvey and Heather Ackroyd do.

00:09:28.212 --> 00:09:29.812
- I think we create these pieces partly

00:09:29.812 --> 00:09:32.612
because we just love actually the magic of

00:09:32.612 --> 00:09:36.372
seeing them in print and
come to life and grow.

00:09:36.372 --> 00:09:38.451
- [Narrator] The British
artists blur the lines

00:09:38.451 --> 00:09:40.692
between science, nature, and art

00:09:40.692 --> 00:09:43.193
by manipulating the nature processes

00:09:43.193 --> 00:09:47.113
that fuel life itself to
create their canvases.

00:09:47.113 --> 00:09:48.873
- [Dan] You're actually imbuing it with

00:09:48.873 --> 00:09:51.692
this energy and creating with life.

00:09:51.692 --> 00:09:54.894
They're almost DayGlo images
when you first see them.

00:09:54.894 --> 00:09:57.294
They're very, very beautiful.

00:09:57.294 --> 00:09:59.877
(instrumental)

00:10:08.996 --> 00:10:10.516
- [Heather] So, we're
standing on Leaf Hill,

00:10:10.516 --> 00:10:13.683
which is near our hometown of Dorking,

00:10:14.553 --> 00:10:16.516
which is about a mile down the road.

00:10:16.516 --> 00:10:19.054
It's an area of
outstanding natural beauty.

00:10:19.054 --> 00:10:21.593
Geologically, the area is green sand.

00:10:21.593 --> 00:10:25.012
Dan as a child used to come
up here with his father.

00:10:25.012 --> 00:10:26.790
- [Dan] Yeah, I was born and bred here

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:28.734
and spent a lot of time up in these woods.

00:10:28.734 --> 00:10:30.473
- [Narrator] In the forest,

00:10:30.473 --> 00:10:32.673
Dan and Heather come
across a group of activists

00:10:32.673 --> 00:10:36.452
protesting plans for oil drilling nearby,

00:10:36.452 --> 00:10:40.132
and decide to document their cause.

00:10:40.132 --> 00:10:42.592
- You're just looking
straight into the lens.

00:10:42.592 --> 00:10:44.390
- [Narrator] They take a
series of photographs with

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:48.713
the hopes that one can be
used for a grass portrait.

00:10:48.713 --> 00:10:51.463
(ethereal music)

00:10:54.473 --> 00:10:55.476
- [Heather] If you actually look at

00:10:55.476 --> 00:10:56.713
what's happened in the last 25 years,

00:10:56.713 --> 00:10:58.331
if you look at what's happened
in the last five years,

00:10:58.331 --> 00:11:00.174
mega flooding scenarios.

00:11:00.174 --> 00:11:02.132
- [Dan] Severe weather events
are happening time and time

00:11:02.132 --> 00:11:05.054
and time again and
increasingly more frequently.

00:11:05.054 --> 00:11:07.232
- And the science is very, very clear

00:11:07.232 --> 00:11:09.796
and unambiguous about this.

00:11:09.796 --> 00:11:12.276
Our work is very much embraced around

00:11:12.276 --> 00:11:15.231
processes of change, around nature,

00:11:15.231 --> 00:11:18.852
around biodiversity loss,
around climate change.

00:11:18.852 --> 00:11:20.836
From the very first time that Dan and I

00:11:20.836 --> 00:11:23.013
started to work together, it was,

00:11:23.013 --> 00:11:25.090
you know, it was within our sight.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:28.549
It's not necessarily a
direct action or activism.

00:11:28.549 --> 00:11:31.092
Sometimes pieces are very, very poetic.

00:11:31.092 --> 00:11:33.759
(uptempo music)

00:11:41.492 --> 00:11:43.673
- [Heather] It's a bit
dark around the eyes.

00:11:43.673 --> 00:11:44.770
That's good, do that one.

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:47.812
I actually think that's really nice.

00:11:47.812 --> 00:11:50.132
We, God, you know, we spend
so much time together.

00:11:50.132 --> 00:11:52.633
You know, it's like 24/7 really, isn't it?

00:11:52.633 --> 00:11:54.974
We work together, live together,
we have a child together.

00:11:54.974 --> 00:11:57.412
We challenge each other's
ideas and thinking,

00:11:57.412 --> 00:11:59.353
but that's really good
because it kind of makes,

00:11:59.353 --> 00:12:02.916
I think it makes our ideas more robust.

00:12:02.916 --> 00:12:04.553
- So I printed these ones off.

00:12:04.553 --> 00:12:06.470
I really like that one.

00:12:07.533 --> 00:12:10.516
- [Heather] I think that's the one.

00:12:10.516 --> 00:12:12.452
- We've been together 26 years.

00:12:12.452 --> 00:12:13.993
- Yeah.

00:12:13.993 --> 00:12:15.770
- And we still, yeah,

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:18.025
when you're a couple who
live and work together,

00:12:18.025 --> 00:12:21.871
the thing is that you actually
really don't switch off.

00:12:21.871 --> 00:12:23.134
- There's just like this kind of charge.

00:12:23.134 --> 00:12:24.770
There's always a charge to what we do.

00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:27.187
(soft music)

00:12:38.367 --> 00:12:39.513
We maybe only do one,

00:12:39.513 --> 00:12:43.332
one or two photosynthesis works a year.

00:12:43.332 --> 00:12:44.852
I think what's interesting is that we're,

00:12:44.852 --> 00:12:48.553
we're not working with lawn
or with sod or with turf.

00:12:48.553 --> 00:12:50.386
We work with the seed.

00:12:55.614 --> 00:12:56.612
- [Narrator] Dan and Heather germinate

00:12:56.612 --> 00:12:58.473
their seeds for about two weeks.

00:12:58.473 --> 00:13:00.873
When ready, they're
rubbed onto the canvas,

00:13:00.873 --> 00:13:03.513
held in place by water paste.

00:13:03.513 --> 00:13:05.689
- [Heather] We're not
carving or sculpting in

00:13:05.689 --> 00:13:07.509
that more traditional sense.

00:13:07.509 --> 00:13:08.971
You know, we're growing.

00:13:08.971 --> 00:13:12.276
- [Dan] The image sort of
develops over the period of weeks.

00:13:12.276 --> 00:13:15.812
To grow one of our pieces,
we grow the grass vertically,

00:13:15.812 --> 00:13:19.476
then we turn the whole of the
studio space into a dark room

00:13:19.476 --> 00:13:22.633
and so the only light
that the grass receives

00:13:22.633 --> 00:13:26.149
as it's growing is from the
projected negative image,

00:13:26.149 --> 00:13:29.097
so where the strongest
light hits the grass,

00:13:29.097 --> 00:13:30.971
it produced more of the chlorophyll,

00:13:30.971 --> 00:13:32.654
more of the green pigment.

00:13:32.654 --> 00:13:34.630
Where there's less light, it's less green.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:36.254
Where there's no light, it grows,

00:13:36.254 --> 00:13:38.052
but it's attenuated in yellow,

00:13:38.052 --> 00:13:40.734
so you get the equivalent to
a black and white photograph,

00:13:40.734 --> 00:13:43.454
but in terms of green to yellow.

00:13:43.454 --> 00:13:44.612
- [Heather] Portraits we tend to do around

00:13:44.612 --> 00:13:46.052
six foot by four foot,

00:13:46.052 --> 00:13:47.913
or maybe seven foot by
five foot, in this case.

00:13:47.913 --> 00:13:49.611
We can work really, really large scale,

00:13:49.611 --> 00:13:51.732
and what's great about
that is that it's not,

00:13:51.732 --> 00:13:54.492
it's not sized a gratuitous way.

00:13:54.492 --> 00:13:56.767
Actually, the resolution we get is

00:13:56.767 --> 00:13:58.552
really, really extraordinary.

00:13:58.552 --> 00:13:59.673
It's phenomenal.

00:13:59.673 --> 00:14:02.313
If you equate a chlorophyll
molecule with a pixel,

00:14:02.313 --> 00:14:04.592
then we're getting kind of
like many more pixels for

00:14:04.592 --> 00:14:07.716
kind of like, you know,
kind of like square foot.

00:14:07.716 --> 00:14:09.294
- The detail you can
get on the large ones is

00:14:09.294 --> 00:14:10.974
absolutely fantastic.

00:14:10.974 --> 00:14:13.156
- [Heather] It's part of a
process of discovery, you know.

00:14:13.156 --> 00:14:15.134
As artists, you're looking
to make discoveries within

00:14:15.134 --> 00:14:17.431
the materials and mediums
that you work with,

00:14:17.431 --> 00:14:21.913
and you know, light and
seeds, water, crystals,

00:14:21.913 --> 00:14:23.833
all of those are kind of
part of our palette of

00:14:23.833 --> 00:14:25.971
materials that we play with.

00:14:25.971 --> 00:14:27.593
- [Narrator] If watered regularly

00:14:27.593 --> 00:14:29.092
and kept at low light levels,

00:14:29.092 --> 00:14:31.812
these grass portraits
can last indefinitely.

00:14:31.812 --> 00:14:33.513
- [Dan] There's something
about seeing the way

00:14:33.513 --> 00:14:37.172
that they grow and they sort
of materialize in front of you,

00:14:37.172 --> 00:14:39.854
really, and you don't
really get to see them until

00:14:39.854 --> 00:14:43.476
the last moment really when
you can put the lights out.

00:14:43.476 --> 00:14:45.893
(soft music)

00:14:50.633 --> 00:14:51.850
- Oh wow.

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:53.350
You grassed me up.

00:14:58.372 --> 00:14:59.412
- [Heather] I feel that this piece has got

00:14:59.412 --> 00:15:02.329
a real strength about it, you know.

00:15:03.209 --> 00:15:06.132
I think the person we
photographed in their youth

00:15:06.132 --> 00:15:10.750
just has this real kind of
connection with the camera

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:13.849
and he's somebody who
cares passionately about

00:15:13.849 --> 00:15:15.605
what he's doing.

00:15:15.605 --> 00:15:16.612
It's like a watercolor.

00:15:16.612 --> 00:15:18.405
The color grazes through it, you know.

00:15:18.405 --> 00:15:20.052
It's very, very beautiful.

00:15:20.052 --> 00:15:23.367
- I'm still a bit taken
aback by how it's grass.

00:15:23.367 --> 00:15:25.353
- [Heather] I know, I know.

00:15:25.353 --> 00:15:26.510
We're really, really pleased with it.

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:29.700
We're really pleased with
the way that it's grown.

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:32.283
(instrumental)

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:38.233
- [Narrator] Sam and Joe
Flanagan are brothers

00:15:38.233 --> 00:15:42.052
who take mountain biking way past extreme.

00:15:42.052 --> 00:15:45.513
- We love doing anything we
can possibly do on our bikes.

00:15:45.513 --> 00:15:47.892
The extremer the better,
the gnarlier the better.

00:15:47.892 --> 00:15:49.492
- [Narrator] They're not too interested in

00:15:49.492 --> 00:15:51.790
well worn paths or typical trails.

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:53.661
- [Flanagan] If it involves
climbing up a snowy hill,

00:15:53.661 --> 00:15:54.806
then so be it.

00:15:54.806 --> 00:15:57.306
That's a good afternoon spent.

00:15:59.991 --> 00:16:01.327
- [Narrator] Instead, they hike and bike

00:16:01.327 --> 00:16:02.690
down some of the highest,

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:03.523
snowiest,

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:08.083
and coldest mountains in Scotland.

00:16:21.554 --> 00:16:24.137
(instrumental)

00:16:25.698 --> 00:16:27.273
- Our childhood was always
really, really active,

00:16:27.273 --> 00:16:28.754
so we'd always be out climbing.

00:16:28.754 --> 00:16:30.751
There's a lot of cavern
around here in Yorkshire.

00:16:30.751 --> 00:16:33.495
- [Sam] Both my parents
were into the outdoors,

00:16:33.495 --> 00:16:36.215
so they got us into sort of
stuff like mountain biking,

00:16:36.215 --> 00:16:39.356
climbing cavern from an early age,

00:16:39.356 --> 00:16:42.156
and we just sort of fell in love with it

00:16:42.156 --> 00:16:44.293
because we lived in a rural area.

00:16:44.293 --> 00:16:47.571
- [Joe] Mountain biking was
just the natural progression.

00:16:47.571 --> 00:16:49.618
- [Sam] The mountain biking we do

00:16:49.618 --> 00:16:51.714
would be classed as extreme because of

00:16:51.714 --> 00:16:54.556
the elements that we do it
in, the places we do it in,

00:16:54.556 --> 00:16:57.116
the kind of stuff we're riding down,

00:16:57.116 --> 00:16:59.378
it's not your average trail center.

00:16:59.378 --> 00:17:01.412
It's steep and it's gnarly and,

00:17:01.412 --> 00:17:03.551
it's just an extra bit of danger,

00:17:03.551 --> 00:17:05.468
which is always a plus.

00:17:22.615 --> 00:17:23.975
- [Narrator] Formed 40 million years ago,

00:17:23.975 --> 00:17:26.556
the Cairngorms is a mountain range in

00:17:26.556 --> 00:17:29.318
the eastern islands of Scotland.

00:17:29.318 --> 00:17:30.498
It is home to five of the six

00:17:30.498 --> 00:17:32.337
highest mountains in the country.

00:17:32.337 --> 00:17:34.236
To say the weather is extreme here

00:17:34.236 --> 00:17:35.975
would be an understatement.

00:17:35.975 --> 00:17:38.839
It's prone to wind speeds
of over 100 miles per hour,

00:17:38.839 --> 00:17:41.036
temperatures well below zero,

00:17:41.036 --> 00:17:44.153
and of course, the occasional avalanche.

00:17:44.153 --> 00:17:48.320
Not exactly the typical
choice for mountain biking.

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:07.740
- It's impossible to
remove the danger from

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:09.235
mountain biking completely

00:18:09.235 --> 00:18:10.860
'cause you're still out in the elements.

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:12.316
There's rocks, there's slippy surfaces.

00:18:12.316 --> 00:18:13.980
You're always gonna
have your little crashes

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:15.397
and your tumbles.

00:18:17.535 --> 00:18:19.373
- The Cairngorms can
be, on a beautiful day,

00:18:19.373 --> 00:18:23.196
can be an extremely rewarding environment

00:18:23.196 --> 00:18:25.596
and really beautiful to behold,

00:18:25.596 --> 00:18:28.939
but in bad conditions
it can be a death zone,

00:18:28.939 --> 00:18:31.477
and many people have died
up in the Cairngorm plateau,

00:18:31.477 --> 00:18:33.419
either being caught out by bad weather

00:18:33.419 --> 00:18:35.336
or falling off a cliff.

00:18:36.856 --> 00:18:39.077
It can be completely, like,

00:18:39.077 --> 00:18:43.244
another world of cold and
extreme weather up there.

00:18:44.199 --> 00:18:45.356
- [Joe] Definitely when it's snowing,

00:18:45.356 --> 00:18:47.500
you need to 100% check the weather.

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:49.340
You need to make sure that
everyone's gonna be safe,

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:50.796
detailed information on what

00:18:50.796 --> 00:18:52.220
the weather's gonna be like up high,

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:53.458
what the wind speeds are gonna be like,

00:18:53.458 --> 00:18:55.036
and it's dangerous.

00:18:55.036 --> 00:18:56.556
There's a lot of people
have to get rescued.

00:18:56.556 --> 00:18:58.334
There's a lot of people, unfortunately,

00:18:58.334 --> 00:19:00.076
are killed in the hills
around the Cairngorms

00:19:00.076 --> 00:19:03.532
'cause they misjudged the weather.

00:19:03.532 --> 00:19:06.449
(electronic music)

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:08.658
One of the main reasons of riding down

00:19:08.658 --> 00:19:11.047
a snowy, icy mountain is
everything's just heightened,

00:19:11.047 --> 00:19:12.460
everything's more slippy,

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:13.956
everything's that little bit more scary.

00:19:13.956 --> 00:19:16.258
Coming down is the best feeling.

00:19:16.258 --> 00:19:17.635
That's where you feel really free,

00:19:17.635 --> 00:19:19.657
just wind blowing through your face

00:19:19.657 --> 00:19:21.719
and carving turns in the snow.

00:19:21.719 --> 00:19:23.479
It's just unmatched.

00:19:23.479 --> 00:19:25.799
Speed-wise, on the snow,
you can pick up, kind of,

00:19:25.799 --> 00:19:28.413
30, 40 miles an hour
without even thinking.

00:19:28.413 --> 00:19:30.359
Stuff just starts blurring past,

00:19:30.359 --> 00:19:32.442
but that's the best part.

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:35.570
A load of adrenaline.

00:19:51.353 --> 00:19:52.887
- [Joe] As my good friend,
Craig Evans always says,

00:19:52.887 --> 00:19:54.391
death before download.

00:19:54.391 --> 00:19:55.596
If you're gonna have to carry down it,

00:19:55.596 --> 00:19:56.812
you're just gonna try it anyway.

00:19:56.812 --> 00:19:59.562
Just get it done, see it through.

00:20:00.636 --> 00:20:03.219
(instrumental)

00:20:18.087 --> 00:20:19.512
- [Narrator] Scattered
across the New Mexico desert

00:20:19.512 --> 00:20:23.679
are buildings that look like
something from another world.

00:20:30.131 --> 00:20:33.115
They're homes designed to be
completely self-sustained,

00:20:33.115 --> 00:20:37.282
generating their own electricity,
water, gas, and food.

00:20:38.496 --> 00:20:41.556
They are the life's work of

00:20:41.556 --> 00:20:44.796
an unconventional
architect, Mike Reynolds,

00:20:44.796 --> 00:20:48.963
who believes homes are the
key to a better future.

00:20:54.291 --> 00:20:55.356
- [Mike] We came up
with the name earthship

00:20:55.356 --> 00:20:58.850
because houses have to
have gas lines coming in,

00:20:58.850 --> 00:21:01.458
sewage lines going out,
electric lines coming in,

00:21:01.458 --> 00:21:05.479
and so these buildings provide
all of that themselves.

00:21:05.479 --> 00:21:07.799
These buildings address
comfortable shelter,

00:21:07.799 --> 00:21:11.966
electricity, water, sewage
treatment, garbage, and food.

00:21:13.655 --> 00:21:15.138
- [Rowan] Mike Reynolds
started all this after

00:21:15.138 --> 00:21:17.996
the oil crisis in the seventies.

00:21:17.996 --> 00:21:19.559
Once the prices went up,

00:21:19.559 --> 00:21:22.292
that meant everything we
had to buy just went up,

00:21:22.292 --> 00:21:24.018
so this house is basically trying to

00:21:24.018 --> 00:21:27.601
make us less dependent
on all these things.

00:21:30.578 --> 00:21:32.333
- [Mike] I came to Taos right after

00:21:32.333 --> 00:21:34.750
architectural school in 1969.

00:21:35.618 --> 00:21:37.596
This climate is super severe.

00:21:37.596 --> 00:21:41.938
It's real hot in the summer and
it's real cold in the winter,

00:21:41.938 --> 00:21:46.098
so it's a perfect place to
test pilot these buildings

00:21:46.098 --> 00:21:49.479
so that you see if they can
stay warm in severe conditions

00:21:49.479 --> 00:21:51.979
and cool in severe conditions.

00:21:53.499 --> 00:21:54.978
- [Narrator] Reynolds’ vision has grown.

00:21:54.978 --> 00:21:59.533
There are now 7,500 people
living in earthships worldwide,

00:21:59.533 --> 00:22:01.557
but constructing the
self-contained buildings

00:22:01.557 --> 00:22:04.156
isn't what you'd call easy.

00:22:04.156 --> 00:22:05.996
It comes down to a complex system of

00:22:05.996 --> 00:22:09.180
sustainable materials in construction.

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:11.436
- [Mike] There are mountains
of automobile tires

00:22:11.436 --> 00:22:12.999
everywhere all over the world.

00:22:12.999 --> 00:22:15.698
Well, why don't we make a use for tires?

00:22:15.698 --> 00:22:19.639
- [Rowan] The tires give out
the foundation of the house

00:22:19.639 --> 00:22:21.556
and also the structural walls.

00:22:21.556 --> 00:22:22.796
- [Mike] The tire will not rot.

00:22:22.796 --> 00:22:24.415
Termites don't eat them.

00:22:24.415 --> 00:22:26.978
They are resilient for earthquakes.

00:22:26.978 --> 00:22:28.476
- [Rowan] And at the same time,

00:22:28.476 --> 00:22:30.556
because they're packed with earth inside,

00:22:30.556 --> 00:22:32.258
it's going to accumulate the heat.

00:22:32.258 --> 00:22:34.338
It's like a heat factory.

00:22:34.338 --> 00:22:36.895
- It sucks up heat and then
it gives it back to you

00:22:36.895 --> 00:22:40.178
when the temperature of the room drops.

00:22:40.178 --> 00:22:42.898
- [Rowan] You do feel really nurtured

00:22:42.898 --> 00:22:45.836
inside some of these houses
because inside the living space,

00:22:45.836 --> 00:22:47.475
the temperature's the same all year round.

00:22:47.475 --> 00:22:50.642
And we have plastic and glass bottles.

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:56.034
We cut them in half and we put
the two bottom ends together.

00:22:56.034 --> 00:22:59.319
It means that we'll be
using much less cement

00:22:59.319 --> 00:23:01.914
because we're using this other
material which compensates.

00:23:01.914 --> 00:23:05.836
At the same time, it's gonna
let light come into the house.

00:23:05.836 --> 00:23:10.003
You can do a combination of
colors, so you can do mosaics.

00:23:14.231 --> 00:23:15.596
- [Narrator] Living off
the grid in the desert

00:23:15.596 --> 00:23:18.258
creates challenges for basic water needs,

00:23:18.258 --> 00:23:20.380
but the answer to this problem is one of

00:23:20.380 --> 00:23:23.719
earthship's most elegant solutions.

00:23:23.719 --> 00:23:25.020
- For the rainfall, we've only got

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:28.217
about eight inches of rain a year,

00:23:28.217 --> 00:23:30.940
so all the roof is dedicated to

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:34.700
recuperate this water and harvest it.

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:37.719
This rainwater, after we've used it once,

00:23:37.719 --> 00:23:39.116
let's say through the shower,

00:23:39.116 --> 00:23:41.178
will go into the botanical cell,

00:23:41.178 --> 00:23:43.339
then it's basically irrigating plants

00:23:43.339 --> 00:23:47.217
and then only after that
does it go into the toilets,

00:23:47.217 --> 00:23:49.856
so we never use the water in the toilets

00:23:49.856 --> 00:23:51.900
directly from the rain.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:54.233
We've already used it twice.

00:24:01.239 --> 00:24:02.796
- [Man] We retired in September of last year.

00:24:02.796 --> 00:24:04.659
We've been here full-time since.

00:24:04.659 --> 00:24:07.826
(soft acoustic music)

00:24:11.613 --> 00:24:13.276
- [Woman] It gives us freedom from bills.

00:24:13.276 --> 00:24:17.099
It gives us the ability to
go back to our natural roots,

00:24:17.099 --> 00:24:19.682
live and love the seasons here.

00:24:22.579 --> 00:24:24.439
We actually got a newsletter
sent to us from Michael,

00:24:24.439 --> 00:24:28.018
and then we decided to
come out here and stay in

00:24:28.018 --> 00:24:29.719
one of the earthships.

00:24:29.719 --> 00:24:33.020
Lo and behold, I saw this
little mud house here for sale,

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:36.020
so that's how we came to this point.

00:24:37.756 --> 00:24:40.437
This community, itself,
this earthship community,

00:24:40.437 --> 00:24:42.279
is full of eclectic people.

00:24:42.279 --> 00:24:45.279
There's people here from everywhere.

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:54.839
- [Rowan] I think that with time,

00:24:54.839 --> 00:24:56.919
we're seeing more and more
people who are interested in it,

00:24:56.919 --> 00:24:59.836
but also who see the
advantage in the long run,

00:24:59.836 --> 00:25:02.236
especially, you know, to
take care of our planet,

00:25:02.236 --> 00:25:04.636
so we want try and have these
bases all around the world

00:25:04.636 --> 00:25:08.636
and be able to continue
training people there directly.

00:25:08.636 --> 00:25:10.060
- [Narrator] Reynolds and his team

00:25:10.060 --> 00:25:12.316
have been sharing their
designs around the world,

00:25:12.316 --> 00:25:16.460
developing earthships in places of need.

00:25:16.460 --> 00:25:19.043
- [Mike] I can't possibly build

00:25:19.916 --> 00:25:23.356
all the buildings that need
to be built on this planet.

00:25:23.356 --> 00:25:25.276
No, I want it to be a virus.

00:25:25.276 --> 00:25:27.916
People get infected, and
they infect more people

00:25:27.916 --> 00:25:29.538
and they infect more people.

00:25:29.538 --> 00:25:31.319
That's the way this can grow.

00:25:31.319 --> 00:25:32.698
- [Narrator] A standard
earthship costs roughly

00:25:32.698 --> 00:25:36.865
$235 per square foot and
comes in a range of sizes.

00:25:38.759 --> 00:25:40.916
- [Mike] I lay in bed
sometimes awake at night,

00:25:40.916 --> 00:25:44.438
can't wait to get up the next
morning and keep doing this.

00:25:44.438 --> 00:25:45.900
We're building, we're drawing,

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:49.356
we're traveling, doing education.

00:25:49.356 --> 00:25:50.359
- [Narrator] Michael Reynolds and his team

00:25:50.359 --> 00:25:52.414
have helped many individuals
and charities build

00:25:52.414 --> 00:25:55.914
these off the grid homes around the world.

00:25:56.876 --> 00:25:58.716
- I plan to keep going until I drop,

00:25:58.716 --> 00:26:02.255
but I'm already seeing that
it doesn't matter when I drop,

00:26:02.255 --> 00:26:05.900
there's people that are
just gonna keep doing this.

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:07.996
- [Narrator] There are roughly
3,000 earthships globally,

00:26:07.996 --> 00:26:10.663
which house around 7,500 people.

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:22.178
- [Aharon] A free diver
to me is a pioneer.

00:26:22.178 --> 00:26:25.159
We're all experimenting in
something that has not been

00:26:25.159 --> 00:26:28.326
the main thrust of humanity until now.

00:26:36.279 --> 00:26:38.236
My name is Aharon Solomons.

00:26:38.236 --> 00:26:40.599
I'm a free-diving instructor trainer.

00:26:40.599 --> 00:26:44.876
I specialize in coaching
athletes who want to do

00:26:44.876 --> 00:26:47.709
national records or world records.

00:26:49.698 --> 00:26:51.319
You've got to kick in your dive reflexes,

00:26:51.319 --> 00:26:54.236
breathe up a little bit, breathing,

00:26:56.199 --> 00:27:00.032
about as you do just
before you went to sleep.

00:27:01.137 --> 00:27:04.387
Then, one big breath, turn over and go.

00:27:10.279 --> 00:27:12.418
The moment that you leave the surface,

00:27:12.418 --> 00:27:16.014
the blood is beginning
to leave the peripheries

00:27:16.014 --> 00:27:19.931
and migrate towards the
so-called noble organs.

00:27:21.499 --> 00:27:24.999
Then, you start to sink faster and faster.

00:27:31.016 --> 00:27:33.453
Once you're free-falling,
it's pure pleasure.

00:27:33.453 --> 00:27:35.870
It's an incredible sensation.

00:27:44.792 --> 00:27:48.149
You’re going down, and the colors change.

00:27:48.149 --> 00:27:50.269
You go down through the clear blue here,

00:27:50.269 --> 00:27:53.769
to a darker blue, and to a purple twilight,

00:27:58.349 --> 00:28:01.516
and then finally into total blackness.

00:28:02.568 --> 00:28:05.603
Then, we have a rude
awakening, hit the plate,

00:28:05.603 --> 00:28:07.331
and then we turn around,

00:28:07.331 --> 00:28:10.989
and we have to work a
bit harder coming up.

00:28:10.989 --> 00:28:12.792
As you come up, you're working less hard

00:28:12.792 --> 00:28:15.568
because buoyancy is returning,

00:28:15.568 --> 00:28:17.985
and then it's like a rebirth.

00:28:19.305 --> 00:28:21.526
You're coming back into the light.

00:28:21.526 --> 00:28:23.304
The whole process is one of pleasure

00:28:23.304 --> 00:28:27.912
and a terrific sense of
adventure and achievement.

00:28:27.912 --> 00:28:30.888
Usually changes and switches.

00:28:30.888 --> 00:28:32.632
All the things that I
thought were impossible,

00:28:32.632 --> 00:28:36.799
all the things that I thought
were not achievable by me are.

00:28:39.389 --> 00:28:43.027
If that's achievable, what
else isn't achievable?

00:28:43.027 --> 00:28:44.644
And that opens a whole Pandora's box,

00:28:44.644 --> 00:28:46.977
so it's quite an experience, free-diving.

00:28:48.712 --> 00:28:51.295
(instrumental)

00:28:55.204 --> 00:28:56.212
- [Narrator] In the shadow of Mauna Loa

00:28:56.212 --> 00:28:58.931
on the island of Hawaii,

00:28:58.931 --> 00:29:03.091
the native Koa tree roots
in shallow volcanic soil.

00:29:03.091 --> 00:29:04.125
- [Male] Acacia Koas are found throughout

00:29:04.125 --> 00:29:06.709
the Hawaiian islands, but nowhere else.

00:29:06.709 --> 00:29:09.274
Today, it is probably the most valuable

00:29:09.274 --> 00:29:12.513
hardwood found anywhere in the world.

00:29:12.513 --> 00:29:14.353
- [Narrator] Acacia Koa
is native to Hawaii.

00:29:14.353 --> 00:29:17.186
Named Koa, meaning brave and bold,

00:29:19.153 --> 00:29:23.697
it is prized for its incredible
beauty, color, and curl.

00:29:23.697 --> 00:29:26.131
It enjoys the humidity of the tropics,

00:29:26.131 --> 00:29:30.497
requiring nearly 200 inches
of rain per year to thrive.

00:29:30.497 --> 00:29:32.433
- We like to leave
these big grandpa trees,

00:29:32.433 --> 00:29:34.609
let them throw seed for the forest,

00:29:34.609 --> 00:29:38.055
and hopefully pass on more
trees for future generations.

00:29:38.055 --> 00:29:40.031
- [Narrator] To honor the
iconic symbol of the islands,

00:29:40.031 --> 00:29:43.857
those who harvest Koa
almost never cut it down,

00:29:43.857 --> 00:29:46.074
allowing mother nature to decide

00:29:46.074 --> 00:29:50.337
when and which to harvest
as the wind blows.

00:29:50.337 --> 00:29:51.393
- It doesn't take much.

00:29:51.393 --> 00:29:53.495
An above average wind will knock

00:29:53.495 --> 00:29:55.697
some pretty large Koa trees down.

00:29:55.697 --> 00:29:59.095
The higher quality instrument grade Koa is

00:29:59.095 --> 00:30:02.012
used for making ukulele and guitar.

00:30:03.633 --> 00:30:06.497
- [Narrator] For 100 years,
the Kamaka family has made

00:30:06.497 --> 00:30:10.828
world-class ukuleles
from these native trees.

00:30:10.828 --> 00:30:12.796
- [Chris] Working with
nature to try and create

00:30:12.796 --> 00:30:16.879
a certain voice is truly
something special to me.

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:19.735
- [Narrator] Inspiring music and musicians

00:30:19.735 --> 00:30:23.652
recognized far and wide
as distinctly Hawaiian.

00:30:24.577 --> 00:30:25.789
- The modern ukulele was truly invented by

00:30:25.789 --> 00:30:27.756
the Kamaka family.

00:30:27.756 --> 00:30:29.457
- [Narrator] Using
techniques passed down for

00:30:29.457 --> 00:30:32.512
four generations, the
Kamakas hope the Koa tree

00:30:32.512 --> 00:30:36.095
will provide another
hundred years of song.

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:48.497
- [Fred] My father never claimed

00:30:48.497 --> 00:30:50.519
to be an ukulele player.

00:30:50.519 --> 00:30:52.212
He made instruments
because his friends did,

00:30:52.212 --> 00:30:54.497
and he took what he learned

00:30:54.497 --> 00:30:57.414
and opened his ukulele guitar shop.

00:30:58.471 --> 00:31:00.471
My father was, he was a perfectionist.

00:31:00.471 --> 00:31:03.909
I remember him saying, "Don't make junk."

00:31:03.909 --> 00:31:07.827
The ukulele became the most
prominent instrument in Hawaii.

00:31:07.827 --> 00:31:11.077
Most businesses don't last beyond maybe

00:31:12.375 --> 00:31:14.542
the first two generations,

00:31:15.894 --> 00:31:18.375
but we've entered the fourth generation.

00:31:18.375 --> 00:31:21.208
We've managed to keep it together.

00:31:38.913 --> 00:31:41.111
- [Casey] The factory has
been a home away from home.

00:31:41.111 --> 00:31:44.028
Everyone here pretty much raised us

00:31:46.369 --> 00:31:47.869
because we were running around the shop

00:31:47.869 --> 00:31:50.192
from when we were young kids.

00:31:50.192 --> 00:31:51.793
You know, in Hawaii,
family's such a big thing,

00:31:51.793 --> 00:31:54.648
and they're part of our family.

00:31:54.648 --> 00:31:56.372
Same like with our musicians.

00:31:56.372 --> 00:31:58.172
Without them, we wouldn't be here,

00:31:58.172 --> 00:32:01.839
and that's how we hope
that they would feel.

00:32:11.815 --> 00:32:13.612
- [Keith] A Koa tree is in the pea family.

00:32:13.612 --> 00:32:17.279
It's a legume, and it's
a pretty hardy tree.

00:32:20.492 --> 00:32:21.991
On our ranch in the Koa zone,

00:32:21.991 --> 00:32:23.789
it starts at 4,000 foot elevation

00:32:23.789 --> 00:32:27.816
and goes up to about 6,500 foot elevation.

00:32:27.816 --> 00:32:29.970
The Koa that we have milled here,

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:33.271
probably some of the most
beautiful Koa I've ever seen.

00:32:33.271 --> 00:32:37.212
Our growing conditions are pretty optimum.

00:32:37.212 --> 00:32:40.290
Kona has a really unique weather pattern.

00:32:40.290 --> 00:32:43.172
Mauna Loa dominates our climate.

00:32:43.172 --> 00:32:45.571
When this huge land mass heats up,

00:32:45.571 --> 00:32:47.932
it draws those ocean currents mauka,

00:32:47.932 --> 00:32:51.415
and as the mountain cools
and nightfall comes,

00:32:51.415 --> 00:32:55.932
the dynamic shifts, and
now this wind blows makai.

00:32:55.932 --> 00:32:58.311
The root system for the Koa is

00:32:58.311 --> 00:33:00.808
shallow-rooted in this volcanic soil.

00:33:00.808 --> 00:33:02.433
Not only is that susceptible to wind,

00:33:02.433 --> 00:33:04.471
but if there's a herd of
cattle that came through here,

00:33:04.471 --> 00:33:06.552
they'd be trampling the
roots and kill the tree.

00:33:06.552 --> 00:33:10.893
Our logging operation is
centered around harvesting

00:33:10.893 --> 00:33:13.953
windfall trees, trees that have fallen,

00:33:13.953 --> 00:33:15.612
and so for the most part,

00:33:15.612 --> 00:33:19.779
we get pretty good business
just with windfall trees.

00:33:22.033 --> 00:33:24.616
(upbeat music)

00:33:26.812 --> 00:33:27.815
- [Casey] I used to bug him all the time,

00:33:27.815 --> 00:33:29.768
"Dad, take me to go look at the wood."

00:33:29.768 --> 00:33:32.753
All I'm doing is envisioning

00:33:32.753 --> 00:33:35.590
this instrument coming out of that log.

00:33:35.590 --> 00:33:39.532
This stack, it's about a
board foot per instrument,

00:33:39.532 --> 00:33:42.935
so that's about 400 ukuleles right here.

00:33:42.935 --> 00:33:45.015
I guess you could say
I'm cheating a little bit

00:33:45.015 --> 00:33:48.750
because I'm using what
mother nature has given us.

00:33:48.750 --> 00:33:49.891
When you build an instrument out of it,

00:33:49.891 --> 00:33:52.628
you're kind of bringing that
tree that you saw up there

00:33:52.628 --> 00:33:55.848
back to life because it's creating music.

00:33:55.848 --> 00:33:57.191
So when the wood finally gets here

00:33:57.191 --> 00:33:58.969
after we've picked it from the big island,

00:33:58.969 --> 00:34:00.231
we dry it out in back.

00:34:00.231 --> 00:34:01.630
It takes at least two years for

00:34:01.630 --> 00:34:04.913
the water naturally to come out.

00:34:04.913 --> 00:34:07.913
From that point, the wood is picked,

00:34:09.828 --> 00:34:12.295
cut, sanded to thickness,

00:34:12.295 --> 00:34:14.878
put into production, it's bent.

00:34:16.012 --> 00:34:17.495
You know, the interior's all braced.

00:34:17.495 --> 00:34:20.871
It's glued together,
where you have the body.

00:34:20.871 --> 00:34:22.371
The neck is built.

00:34:24.513 --> 00:34:27.263
Once that's built, it's glued on.

00:34:31.490 --> 00:34:34.513
We front the instrument,
and then it goes into

00:34:34.513 --> 00:34:39.052
finish process of filling
and spraying lacquer on it.

00:34:39.052 --> 00:34:41.271
Once it's dried, we glue the bridge on,

00:34:41.271 --> 00:34:44.612
tuners, set up, get the action correct,

00:34:44.612 --> 00:34:47.362
and then it's ready to be played.

00:34:51.431 --> 00:34:55.757
- My dad started his
ukulele shop as a hobby,

00:34:55.757 --> 00:34:58.681
and it's been going for 100 years.

00:34:58.681 --> 00:35:00.098
- Hey, what's up?

00:35:01.417 --> 00:35:04.020
- We love to hear the good
sound of the ukuleles.

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:08.260
Creating that has been
a lifetime of service

00:35:08.260 --> 00:35:11.097
to the people of the islands.

00:35:11.097 --> 00:35:15.097
100 years is a long time
keeping people singing.

00:35:23.013 --> 00:35:25.161
- [Jake] The ukulele is a
symbol of aloha, you know.

00:35:25.161 --> 00:35:29.880
It represents Hawaii; it
represents the culture here.

00:35:29.880 --> 00:35:31.961
I get to take the ukulele
all around the world

00:35:31.961 --> 00:35:35.838
to share a little bit of Hawaii
with the rest of the world,

00:35:35.838 --> 00:35:38.156
and it's such a beautiful thing.

00:35:38.156 --> 00:35:40.617
For me, there's a very
unique sound about it,

00:35:40.617 --> 00:35:43.518
and when you hear it, you
know, it just makes you smile.

00:35:43.518 --> 00:35:45.797
I wouldn't be the player I am today

00:35:45.797 --> 00:35:48.797
if it weren't for their instruments.

00:35:56.134 --> 00:35:57.140
- [Narrator] Iceland is home to

00:35:57.140 --> 00:35:58.377
the largest glacier in Europe,

00:35:58.377 --> 00:36:02.878
and an abundance of wild
freezing cold water,

00:36:02.878 --> 00:36:05.056
which makes for a unique
fishing experience for

00:36:05.056 --> 00:36:07.056
those willing to try it.

00:36:09.700 --> 00:36:12.137
Stjani Ben is a fly-fishing guide

00:36:12.137 --> 00:36:16.041
who doesn't hesitate to wade
into waist deep glacial waters

00:36:16.041 --> 00:36:18.708
all for the thrill of the catch.

00:36:20.537 --> 00:36:21.817
- [Stjani] Favorite
part is always the take,

00:36:21.817 --> 00:36:23.814
you know, when the fish takes the fly.

00:36:23.814 --> 00:36:25.897
Adrenaline just goes off.

00:36:28.078 --> 00:36:30.820
Just casting, minding your own business,

00:36:30.820 --> 00:36:32.660
and all of a sudden, boom!

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:34.327
That's a lot of fun.

00:36:41.657 --> 00:36:42.777
We're at Tungufljót,

00:36:42.777 --> 00:36:47.537
which is a sea trout river on
the south coast of Iceland.

00:36:47.537 --> 00:36:50.201
This river is known for very big fish.

00:36:50.201 --> 00:36:52.820
I think it's because of all the glaciers,

00:36:52.820 --> 00:36:56.987
and this is fairly new geologically
of this part of the country.

00:36:58.958 --> 00:37:00.318
The river's not very fertile,

00:37:00.318 --> 00:37:02.521
and that's why we have sea trout in there.

00:37:02.521 --> 00:37:04.521
They have to go to sea
to get something to eat,

00:37:04.521 --> 00:37:08.438
and there's not a lot of
people who enjoy this.

00:37:10.361 --> 00:37:12.820
It's fun being fishing
in this kind of weather.

00:37:12.820 --> 00:37:14.519
We're breaking ice off
the rock all the time,

00:37:14.519 --> 00:37:18.775
you know, because wind
chill, and the rock gets wet.

00:37:18.775 --> 00:37:20.340
Once you have your first fish,

00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:24.978
it, it starts to be a lot more
about nature, the river,

00:37:24.978 --> 00:37:27.438
getting away from your normal life.

00:37:27.438 --> 00:37:31.605
I don't know, just being
outside is what it's all about.

00:37:35.417 --> 00:37:37.471
Fly-fishing offers a never
ending journey, so to speak,

00:37:37.471 --> 00:37:39.893
although it sounds cliché.

00:37:39.893 --> 00:37:42.537
You're never gonna master it,
no matter how hard you try,

00:37:42.537 --> 00:37:44.276
no matter how much time
you spend doing it.

00:37:44.276 --> 00:37:46.676
You're never, ever gonna master it.

00:37:46.676 --> 00:37:50.426
It offers nonstop
challenges, new challenges.

00:37:59.076 --> 00:38:01.493
(bird calls)

00:38:07.496 --> 00:38:08.499
- [Wolfgang] When I was younger,

00:38:08.499 --> 00:38:10.820
I used to think that
architecture and art and science

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:13.998
were all completely different disciplines,

00:38:13.998 --> 00:38:15.577
and the older I've got,

00:38:15.577 --> 00:38:17.577
I've come to realize there's
a lot of similarities.

00:38:17.577 --> 00:38:18.836
There's some gray areas I think

00:38:18.836 --> 00:38:21.035
between kind of art and architecture.

00:38:21.035 --> 00:38:22.420
- [Narrator] The Hive is an installation

00:38:22.420 --> 00:38:25.038
created by artist Wolfgang Buttress.

00:38:25.038 --> 00:38:28.257
It's been built in the Royal
Botanical Gardens in London.

00:38:28.257 --> 00:38:29.657
- [Wolfgang] I wanted it to
fit really comfortably in

00:38:29.657 --> 00:38:31.817
its landscape, so it
almost looks like a swarm,

00:38:31.817 --> 00:38:35.220
a swarm of bees, and then
the closer you get to it,

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:37.557
it reveals its sort of structure.

00:38:37.557 --> 00:38:40.356
The sense of kind of moving from chaos

00:38:40.356 --> 00:38:42.756
to the kind of, the order and the beauty

00:38:42.756 --> 00:38:44.740
and the harmony of a honey comb.

00:38:44.740 --> 00:38:46.115
- [Narrator] The lights
and sounds inside The Hive

00:38:46.115 --> 00:38:49.915
are triggered by the
activity of a real bee hive.

00:38:49.915 --> 00:38:51.177
The busier the bee hive,

00:38:51.177 --> 00:38:55.138
the more intense the light and sounds.

00:38:55.138 --> 00:38:56.217
- [Richard] So I first
heard about The Hive,

00:38:56.217 --> 00:38:57.835
I read about it actually in the newspaper,

00:38:57.835 --> 00:39:00.034
and the purpose of The Hive
is to tell the story of

00:39:00.034 --> 00:39:02.553
the role of bees in pollinating crop lots

00:39:02.553 --> 00:39:04.820
and therefore feeding the planet,

00:39:04.820 --> 00:39:05.732
and I thought that was perfect for Kew,

00:39:05.732 --> 00:39:08.692
because we want to bring
alive why plants matter,

00:39:08.692 --> 00:39:10.089
and clearly one of the
most important things

00:39:10.089 --> 00:39:13.172
that plants do is they feed humanity.

00:39:17.967 --> 00:39:19.406
- [Narrator] Dr. Bencsik
placed an accelerometer

00:39:19.406 --> 00:39:21.323
inside a real bee hive.

00:39:22.233 --> 00:39:23.989
These accelerometers
measure the vibrations

00:39:23.989 --> 00:39:26.239
and energy of the bee hive.

00:39:30.312 --> 00:39:31.647
The hive is connected to the sculpture,

00:39:31.647 --> 00:39:35.814
and its activity is expressed
through light and sound.

00:39:44.350 --> 00:39:46.990
- The Hive itself is
really telling a message

00:39:46.990 --> 00:39:49.630
about why bees are important.

00:39:49.630 --> 00:39:50.644
They're important to society

00:39:50.644 --> 00:39:52.188
because they help pollinate food

00:39:52.188 --> 00:39:53.849
or provide the food that we eat,

00:39:53.849 --> 00:39:55.790
but they also are a very important part of

00:39:55.790 --> 00:39:58.123
our ecology, our ecosystems.

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:02.393
- [Wolfgang] The honey
bee's responsible for

00:40:02.393 --> 00:40:07.209
30% of all the food that we
eat, but the bee is in crisis.

00:40:07.209 --> 00:40:09.591
It's dying in unprecedented numbers

00:40:09.591 --> 00:40:13.758
because of climate change,
pesticides, lack of biodiversity.

00:40:16.270 --> 00:40:19.550
I really hope the visitor will
have an emotional experience,

00:40:19.550 --> 00:40:23.209
you know, something which kind of
connects them to nature.

00:40:23.209 --> 00:40:25.812
I think sometimes you can
say a lot more by being quiet

00:40:25.812 --> 00:40:29.310
and sometimes we forget how
powerful important sounds

00:40:29.310 --> 00:40:31.828
and touch and smell are, so to me,

00:40:31.828 --> 00:40:33.652
that was really important
that these elements

00:40:33.652 --> 00:40:36.425
were at the heart of the installation.

00:40:36.425 --> 00:40:37.982
- [Narrator] Thousands of
people continue to flock to

00:40:37.982 --> 00:40:41.815
Kew Gardens to view and
experience this 40 ton

00:40:43.172 --> 00:40:47.339
steel honeycomb, an ode to
the plight of honey bees.

